{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nPydantic deliberately counts one Agent-visible model turn even when `FallbackModel` tries multiple candidates inside it. The approved RP-01 decision follows maintained-project practice: keep that request budget authoritative and make candidate activity operationally visible as tracing, not as `candidate_requests` in usage. `Agent.instrument_all()` (already called by `setup_tracing`, `src/rebar/llm/tracing.py:54`) instruments only the Agent's OUTER model — for a fallback run that is the `FallbackModel`, yielding ONE model-request span whose internal candidate iteration is invisible. Candidate construction is centralized in `model_classes.build_fallback_model` (`src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py:414+`); `runner.PydanticAIRunner` already records ordered candidate provenance (`src/rebar/llm/runner.py:372-465`). This story is purely additive instrumentation and does NOT change the S2 `_pai_structured` control flow, whose one bounded operation already preserves the bug-895c native-to-prompted fallback (S2 AC-7).\n\n## Approach\nMake each candidate visible by wrapping every already-built concrete candidate model (inside `build_fallback_model`) in a thin subclass of pydantic-ai's own `pydantic_ai.models.wrapper.WrapperModel`. Its `request` override opens ONE rebar-owned child span — via `opentelemetry.trace.get_tracer(...).start_as_current_span(...)` on the SAME `TracerProvider` that `setup_tracing`/`instrument_all` already configures (not a parallel tracer) — around `super().request(...)`. It reuses the library's wrapping primitive but does NOT use `InstrumentedModel` directly, because `InstrumentedModel` owns its request-span lifecycle with no public hook to attach rebar's per-candidate `order`/`outcome`; a rebar-owned child gives that control. Nesting is by ambient OTel context: `instrument_all`'s outer `InstrumentedModel` span is current when `FallbackModel.request` calls each candidate, so the child span parents to it automatically — and because `instrument_all` does NOT re-wrap inner candidates, exactly one rebar span is emitted per candidate (no duplication). A short SPIKE confirms both the child-under-parent nesting and the no-double-wrap property before implementation.\n\nEach candidate span records: zero-based `order`, provider-qualified model identity, an `outcome` from the closed vocabulary {`returned`,`raised`}, and provider-reported usage only when the SDK response exposes it (else omitted, never inferred). Any error text on a `raised` span is placed under the allowlisted `message` key and taken back out sanitized — `sanitize_diagnostic({\"message\": reason})[\"message\"]` — so no prompt, response, credential, endpoint, or unsanitized body reaches the span. The bug-895c native-to-prompted downgrade needs NO code change here: both the native and prompted `agent.run` route through the SAME wrapped candidates, so the downgrade is already legible as the native attempt's span `raised` (sanitized reason) then the prompted attempt's span `returned`. Instrumentation is best-effort: any exception in candidate wrapping or span emission — tracing enabled or disabled, exporter present or absent — is swallowed at the wrapper boundary so the fallback result, `RunUsage`, and the propagated exception/`FallbackExceptionGroup` are identical to tracing-off. Do not alter `FallbackModel` selection, `ProviderSession` lifecycle, `RunUsage`, `UsageLimits`, pricing, JSONL, or the S2 `_pai_structured` control flow. Provider SDK/HTTP retries stay outside this candidate layer and are never inferred.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py` — wrap concrete candidate models in the `WrapperModel` subclass at construction, without changing order or fallback predicates.\n- `src/rebar/llm/tracing.py` — define the `WrapperModel`-based candidate-span helper: the shared-tracer accessor, the bounded rebar attributes (`order`, model identity, `outcome`, optional usage), and the best-effort swallow.\n- `src/rebar/llm/runner.py` — thread candidate order/provenance into the wrapper without adding retry policy.\n- `tests/unit/test_llm_fallback_chain.py`, `tests/unit/test_agent_call_seam.py` — candidate order, outcome, provenance, and usage non-interference.\n- `tests/unit/test_candidate_tracing.py` — new focused module using an in-memory OTel span exporter, plus the lean-import and tracing-ON span-failure contracts.\n\n## Testing\n- Primary 503 then fallback success: one Agent model-request span parenting two ordered candidate spans (`raised` then `returned`).\n- All candidates fail: one `raised` span per attempted candidate; the existing ordered `FallbackExceptionGroup` unchanged.\n- Bug-895c downgrade: native attempt span `raised` (sanitized reason) then prompted attempt span `returned`, with no `_pai_structured` change.\n- Secret-canary: a candidate whose error carries an API key/email yields a `raised` span whose reason is redacted and which holds no prompt/response/endpoint body.\n- Tracing ENABLED with a span-emission/exporter failure: result, usage, and propagated exception identical to tracing-off (span error swallowed).\n- Tracing disabled: identical result, usage, imports, and exception behavior.\n- Lean-import subprocess: base install without the tracing extra imports and runs with no span dependency error.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each attempted fallback candidate emits exactly one rebar OTel span, ordered zero-based, with no duplicate from `instrument_all`; verified by an in-memory exporter test.\n- [ ] Every candidate span parents to the single Agent model-request span (ambient-context nesting); verified by parent-span-id assertions.\n- [ ] Each candidate span carries provider-qualified model identity; verified by attribute assertions.\n- [ ] Each candidate span carries an `outcome` drawn only from {`returned`,`raised`}: the answering model `returned`, each earlier failed candidate `raised`; verified by attribute assertions.\n- [ ] The existing ordered `FallbackExceptionGroup` and resolution classification are unchanged by instrumentation; verified by fallback-chain tests.\n- [ ] The S2-preserved bug-895c native-to-prompted fallback is legible as a native attempt `raised` then a prompted attempt `returned`, with no change to the `_pai_structured` control flow; verified by a downgrade trace test.\n- [ ] A `raised` span reason is redacted through `sanitize_diagnostic` (keyed under `message`) and contains no prompt, response, credential, endpoint secret, or unsanitized exception body; verified by secret-canary assertions.\n- [ ] A span-emission or exporter failure while tracing is ENABLED leaves the fallback result, `RunUsage`, and propagated exception identical to tracing-off; verified by a tracing-ON span-failure test.\n- [ ] `_usage`, `UsageLimits`, pricing, and durable usage JSONL are identical with tracing on and off and contain no candidate counter; verified by paired-result assertions.\n- [ ] The base install without the tracing extra imports and runs with no span dependency error; verified by a lean-import subprocess test."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786963035820920001,"uuid":"8ac21fa1-c3af-4686-b1ca-5a6d46f963d7"}